ProsperoSunshine: a request handler can reach the socket of its own connection

Sunshine's GameStream HTTPS server decides per paired device what it may do (hidden apps, "PS5 Screen", profiles), by the
client certificate. Request keeps its connection private, so Sunshine took the certificate from a process-wide value that the
last TLS handshake set, which another client's handshake could overwrite before the request arrived. Request::socket() hands
out the connection's socket (an SSL stream for HTTPS), so a handler reads the certificate of the connection its request came
on. It shares ownership of the connection while held, like the session that runs the handler.

--- a/server_http.hpp
+++ b/server_http.hpp
@@ -271,6 +271,17 @@
         return asio::ip::tcp::endpoint();
       }
 
+      /// The socket of the connection this request came on, for what only the transport knows, such as the certificate a TLS
+      /// client presented in its handshake. The pointer keeps the connection alive while it is held; nullptr once the
+      /// connection is gone.
+      std::shared_ptr<socket_type> socket() const noexcept {
+        if(auto connection = this->connection.lock()) {
+          if(connection->socket)
+            return std::shared_ptr<socket_type>(connection, connection->socket.get());
+        }
+        return nullptr;
+      }
+
